<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:app="clr-namespace:ChronoMock.App">

  <!--
    THE PARTS LIBRARY. Everything a screen is built from, drawn by us rather than by the toolkit.

    🔴 THIS DICTIONARY IS NOT MERGED INTO App.xaml YET, and that is deliberate. The styles here are
    implicit (keyed by type), so merging them application-wide would restyle every existing screen the
    moment the file exists. Until the screens are rebuilt, only the component catalogue loads this,
    which keeps the shipped views pixel-identical while the parts are designed. Adopting it later is
    ONE line in App.xaml, for the whole application at once.

    WHY DRAW THEM AT ALL. A toolkit control renders itself its own way and no amount of theming changes
    the drawing - same colour, same height, same font, different shape. That is a decision with a real
    price (this is code to maintain for as long as the product lives) and it was the owner's to make.

    THE LANGUAGE OF STATES, and it is one language across every part here:
      the cursor is here       -> the FILL moves one step
      it is being pressed      -> the fill moves the other way, a recess
      the keyboard is here     -> a RING appears outside the control
      it cannot be used        -> the edge goes, the ink dims
    Cursor and keyboard answer on different axes on purpose, so a control that is hovered AND focused
    still says both things at once.

    🔴 NO PART CHANGES ITS LABEL COLOUR ON HOVER. That is a standing rule, and it is also why the accent
    fill moves rather than the ink on it: the dark label holds 8.56, 9.93 and 7.19 across rest, hover
    and press, so it never has to.

    Values: every colour, gap, radius and size here comes from Colours.xaml or Values.xaml. The literal
    guard scans this file - a raw number in it is a build failure, not a style choice.
  -->

  <!--
    ============================ Focus ring ============================

    One ring, drawn OUTSIDE the control with a gap of background between them, reused by every part.

    🔴 Why outside rather than on the edge. The accent fill and the ring are the same hue by design, and
    the hovered accent fill sits 1.02 from the ring - a ring drawn on that edge disappears into it, and
    no shade fixes it while both stay this hue. Outside, the ring is measured against the surface behind
    the control instead: 10.08 on the window, 8.84 on a card.

    It is also why the ring carries a negative margin rather than padding the control: a focused control
    must not change size, or every neighbour shifts when the keyboard arrives.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartFocusRing" TargetType="Border">
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushFocusRing}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource FocusRingThickness}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusMd}" />
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource FocusRingMargin}" />
    <Setter Property="IsHitTestVisible" Value="False" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Card ============================

    The one container for content. No border: it is told apart from the window by its fill alone, which
    is the distance every dark interface uses for this (1.14 here, GitHub 1.09, VS Code 1.08). Borders
    are reserved for controls, so a rectangle with an edge always means "you can act on this".
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartCard" TargetType="Border">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushBgSurface}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusMd}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource CardPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Pinned footer ============================

    Where a screen's action lives, held at the bottom while everything above it scrolls.

    🔴 THE REASON IS MEASURED, not stylistic. The setup phase comes to around 710 px, the window opens at
    800 and may be dragged down to 360, so the one button the screen exists for was below the fold and
    had to be hunted for. A footer does not make the screen shorter - it makes the action reachable
    without knowing there is more of it.

    It carries the base background rather than the card's, because content has to disappear UNDER it
    rather than through it, and the single top edge is what says the scrolling stopped here. An all-round
    border would make it a box, and it is not a box.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartFooter" TargetType="Border">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushBgBase}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushDivider}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource FooterEdge}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource CardPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Type roles ============================

    Named text roles, so a view says what a piece of text IS rather than how big it is. Sizes come from
    the five-step scale and nowhere else.

    🔴 THREE ROLES, AND THE SCREEN HAS TO USE ALL THREE. Measured on the first setup render: 13 labels,
    19 captions and NOT ONE heading, so a section title and the field label under it were the same size,
    the same weight and the same ink. There was nothing left to tell a reader which level they were
    looking at, and the whole screen read as one list of equal things.

    It is called a heading rather than a section heading because it is one step above body wherever that
    step is needed - the title of a group, and the answer a footer exists to state. Two names for one
    appearance is the same fault as one name for two, in the other direction.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartHeading" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeSubtitle}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource FontWeightHeading}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <Style x:Key="PartLabel" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <Style x:Key="PartCaption" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeCaption}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    🔴 PROSE IS NOT A CAPTION, and one role was doing both jobs. A caption is metadata standing beside a
    value - "speed:", a chip, the zone under a clock - and 12 px of quiet ink is right for it. An
    explanation is the text that TEACHES the tool: why the date says 2038, what an option costs, what
    stopping does, what a warning means. Measured on the two phases: 32 of 54 text elements were captions,
    and about eighteen of those were whole sentences.

    Written the wrong way round, that text took TWO reductions at once - smaller AND quieter - and the
    measure in the suite could not see it: every reading passes WCAG at 7.3 to 8.6 against a required 4.5.
    The ratio was never the problem. The size was.

    So this is body size in the caption's ink: quieter than a label, exactly as large. The ink is
    deliberately unchanged - held up beside another tool whose secondary text reads well, its grey is no
    lighter than ours and often darker, and what it does differently is keep that text the same size as
    the text above it.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartNote" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    🔴 THE ONE WORD A FINISHED SESSION LEADS WITH, a step above the heading because it is the peak of its
    screen: the result phase has no clocks, so nothing else on it may be this size. The caller sets the
    ink from the meaning palette and pairs the word with a glyph - colour never carries the meaning alone
    (zasady/13 section 9) - and the role itself knows nothing of verdicts, so the same drawing serves
    "Works" and "Did not start" alike.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartVerdictHeadline" TargetType="TextBlock">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeTitle}" />
    <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource FontWeightHeading}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Button ============================

    Implicit: every button on a screen that loads this dictionary is drawn by this template. A second
    look (the primary action) is a VARIANT below, driven by a property, never a copy of the template.
  -->
  <Style TargetType="Button">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlRest}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlBorder}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ControlHeight}" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource ControlMinWidth}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource ControlPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <!-- The toolkit's dotted rectangle is replaced by the ring in the template, so it is turned off
         here rather than left to draw a second, different focus indicator on top of ours. -->
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="Button">
          <Grid>
            <Border x:Name="Ring" Style="{StaticResource PartFocusRing}" Visibility="Collapsed" />
            <Border x:Name="Face"
                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="{StaticResource StrokeThinEdge}"
                    CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                    Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                                HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                                VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                                RecognizesAccessKey="True" />
            </Border>
          </Grid>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <!-- The keyboard answers on the ring, and only the keyboard: a ring that also appeared on
                 mouse focus would say "you can type here" after every click. The ring is identical for
                 every variant, so it is the one state that belongs to the drawing rather than the style. -->
            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>

    <!--
      🔴 State lives in the STYLE, not in the template, and that is what makes a variant a parameter
      instead of a copy. The face binds Background and BorderBrush from the control, so a variant only
      restates those two values per state - it never restates the drawing. The first draft of this file
      did copy the whole template for the primary action, which is the same mistake as a second style
      sheet: two drawings of one shape, drifting apart from the day they exist.
    -->
    <Style.Triggers>
      <!-- The cursor answers on fill. The label is untouched, here and everywhere. -->
      <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlHover}" />
      </Trigger>
      <Trigger Property="IsPressed" Value="True">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlPressed}" />
      </Trigger>

      <!--
        🔴 THE ACCENT IS A STATE, NOT A SECOND BUTTON. It marks the thing to do next, and it lives on the
        ordinary button so a screen can move it as the answer changes - an empty target makes Choose the
        step, a filled one makes Start the step - without either control changing which style it wears.
        See PartState.IsSuggested.

        Ordered after the plain cursor states and before the disabled one, deliberately: the last matching
        trigger wins, so a suggested button gets accent hover rather than grey hover, and a disabled one
        goes quiet whatever it was suggesting.

        The ink is DARK on this fill, which is a property of the hue rather than a preference: 8.56 against
        white ink's 2.01, on a floor of 4.5. White on accent is closed for this application.
      -->
      <Trigger Property="app:PartState.IsSuggested" Value="True">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccent}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccent}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushOnAccent}" />
      </Trigger>
      <MultiTrigger>
        <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
          <Condition Property="app:PartState.IsSuggested" Value="True" />
          <Condition Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True" />
        </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccentHover}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccentHover}" />
      </MultiTrigger>
      <MultiTrigger>
        <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
          <Condition Property="app:PartState.IsSuggested" Value="True" />
          <Condition Property="IsPressed" Value="True" />
        </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccentPressed}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccentPressed}" />
      </MultiTrigger>

      <!--
        No invitation, but still a control.

        🔴 The first version removed the edge entirely and put the card's own fill behind it. Rendered,
        the button VANISHED - fill equal to the card and no border leaves nothing to see, and "cannot be
        used" had turned into "is not there". A disabled control still has to be findable, or the screen
        silently loses a row.

        So the edge stays and goes QUIET instead: the divider step, 1.34 against a card, which reads as
        present-but-inactive rather than absent. The fill does not move at all, which is the second half
        of the fix - every variant now says "disabled" the same way, by dimming, and none of them says it
        by disappearing.

        🔴 The fill IS restated here, and only because of the suggested state above: for a plain button
        this setter changes nothing, while without it a disabled suggestion would keep shouting in the
        accent colour. That is the third time this library has had to be taught the same lesson, after the
        check control and the choice row.
      -->
      <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlRest}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushDivider}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
      </Trigger>
    </Style.Triggers>
  </Style>

  <!--
    The primary action. One per screen. It is the same template with a size nothing else has, because an
    accent fill alone reads as decoration on a control shaped exactly like its neighbours.

    🔴 IT NO LONGER OWNS THE ACCENT, it only declares itself suggested and lets the base style draw it.
    The variant used to carry its own copy of the four accent values, which was fine while the accent was
    the property of one control - and stopped being fine the moment a screen needed the accent to MOVE,
    to Choose while there is no target and to Start once there is. Two copies of one look drift the first
    time either is touched, so there is now one, in PartState.IsSuggested's trigger above.

    What is left here is the part that really is the variant: it is bigger.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartPrimaryAction" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type Button}}">
    <Setter Property="app:PartState.IsSuggested" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="MinWidth" Value="{StaticResource PrimaryActionMinWidth}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Text field ============================

    🔴 A field shows its ring on ANY focus, where a button shows it on keyboard focus only. That is not
    an inconsistency in the language, it is the language being about meaning: on a field the ring says
    "your typing lands here", which is true however the caret arrived. On a button the same mark would
    say the same thing and be a lie.

    The error state moves the EDGE, not the fill. A tinted fill would change the surface the typed text
    is read on, which is the one thing a field must not do while somebody is fixing a mistake on it.
  -->
  <Style TargetType="TextBox">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlRest}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlBorder}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="CaretBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="SelectionBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccent}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ControlHeight}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource ControlPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="TextBox">
          <Grid>
            <Border x:Name="Ring" Style="{StaticResource PartFocusRing}" Visibility="Collapsed" />
            <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="{StaticResource StrokeThinEdge}"
                    CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <Grid>
                <!-- 🔴 UNDER the text host, not instead of it, and inset by the SAME padding: the hint
                     has to start on the exact x the caret will, or it reads as a value already in the
                     field. It never takes the mouse, so a click lands on the field beneath it. -->
                <TextBlock x:Name="Placeholder"
                           Text="{Binding Path=(app:PartState.Placeholder), RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}"
                           Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                           VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                           Foreground="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           IsHitTestVisible="False"
                           Visibility="Collapsed" />
                <ScrollViewer x:Name="PART_ContentHost"
                              Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                              VerticalAlignment="{TemplateBinding VerticalContentAlignment}"
                              Focusable="False"
                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden"
                              VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Hidden" />
              </Grid>
            </Border>
          </Grid>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsFocused" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <!-- Empty is the whole condition, focus is not part of it. See PartState.Placeholder. -->
            <Trigger Property="Text" Value="">
              <Setter TargetName="Placeholder" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
    <Style.Triggers>
      <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlHover}" />
      </Trigger>
      <Trigger Property="app:PartState.HasError" Value="True">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushError}" />
      </Trigger>
      <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushDivider}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
      </Trigger>
    </Style.Triggers>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Check ============================

    The box carries the state, the label never moves and never changes colour. Checked is the accent
    fill with a dark tick on it, which is the same pairing the primary action uses - one accent meaning
    one thing across the whole interface.
  -->
  <Style TargetType="CheckBox">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="CheckBox">
          <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Background="Transparent">
            <Grid VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <Border x:Name="Ring" Style="{StaticResource PartFocusRing}" Visibility="Collapsed" />
              <Border x:Name="Box"
                      Width="{StaticResource CheckBoxSize}" Height="{StaticResource CheckBoxSize}"
                      Background="{DynamicResource BrushControlRest}"
                      BorderBrush="{DynamicResource BrushControlBorder}"
                      BorderThickness="{StaticResource StrokeThinEdge}"
                      CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                      SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                <Path x:Name="Tick"
                      Width="{StaticResource CheckTickSize}" Height="{StaticResource CheckTickSize}"
                      Stretch="Uniform"
                      Data="M 0,5 L 4,9 L 11,1"
                      Stroke="{DynamicResource BrushOnAccent}"
                      StrokeThickness="{StaticResource CheckTickStroke}"
                      StrokeStartLineCap="Round" StrokeEndLineCap="Round" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                      Visibility="Collapsed" />
              </Border>
            </Grid>
            <ContentPresenter x:Name="Label"
                              Margin="{StaticResource CheckLabelGap}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              RecognizesAccessKey="True" />
          </StackPanel>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsChecked" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Box" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccent}" />
              <Setter TargetName="Box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushAccent}" />
              <Setter TargetName="Tick" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushGlyphMuted}" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
              <Setter TargetName="Box" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushDivider}" />
              <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
            </Trigger>
            <!-- Checked AND disabled: the fill has to dim too, or a dead control still shouts in the
                 accent colour and reads as the most important thing on the screen. -->
            <MultiTrigger>
              <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                <Condition Property="IsChecked" Value="True" />
                <Condition Property="IsEnabled" Value="False" />
              </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
              <Setter TargetName="Box" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlPressed}" />
              <Setter TargetName="Tick" Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
            </MultiTrigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Drop-down ============================

    The hardest part to draw, because it is three things at once: a face that shows the current choice,
    a button that opens, and a floating list. All three answer in the same language as everything else.

    🔴 The toggle inside it carries NO look of its own. It is a transparent hit area over the face, so
    the control has one edge and one fill rather than a control with a button sitting in it - which is
    what a toolkit drop-down looks like, and one of the things that makes a screen look assembled from
    parts rather than designed.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartDropDownToggle" TargetType="ToggleButton">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False" />
    <Setter Property="ClickMode" Value="Press" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="ToggleButton">
          <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}" SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <Style TargetType="ComboBox">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlRest}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlBorder}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ControlHeight}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource ControlPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalContentAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="ComboBox">
          <Grid>
            <Border x:Name="Ring" Style="{StaticResource PartFocusRing}" Visibility="Collapsed" />
            <Border x:Name="Face"
                    Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                    BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                    BorderThickness="{StaticResource StrokeThinEdge}"
                    CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                    SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
              <Grid>
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                  <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="0"
                                  Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                  Content="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItem}"
                                  ContentTemplate="{TemplateBinding SelectionBoxItemTemplate}"
                                  VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                  HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                  IsHitTestVisible="False" />

                <Path x:Name="Chevron" Grid.Column="1"
                      Width="{StaticResource ChevronSize}" Height="{StaticResource ChevronSize}"
                      Margin="{StaticResource ChevronGap}"
                      Stretch="Uniform"
                      Data="M 1,1 L 6,6 L 11,1"
                      Stroke="{DynamicResource BrushGlyphMuted}"
                      StrokeThickness="{StaticResource CheckTickStroke}"
                      StrokeStartLineCap="Round" StrokeEndLineCap="Round" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                      VerticalAlignment="Center"
                      IsHitTestVisible="False" />

                <ToggleButton Grid.Column="0" Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                              Style="{StaticResource PartDropDownToggle}"
                              IsChecked="{Binding IsDropDownOpen, Mode=TwoWay, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
              </Grid>
            </Border>

            <!-- The list floats above everything, so it needs its own surface and its own edge: it is
                 the one place where a border does not mean "you can act on this" but "this is not part
                 of the screen behind it". -->
            <Popup x:Name="PART_Popup"
                   AllowsTransparency="True"
                   Placement="Bottom"
                   Focusable="False"
                   PopupAnimation="None"
                   IsOpen="{Binding IsDropDownOpen, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}">
              <Border Background="{DynamicResource BrushBgSurface}"
                      BorderBrush="{DynamicResource BrushControlBorder}"
                      BorderThickness="{StaticResource StrokeThinEdge}"
                      CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                      Margin="{StaticResource PopupGap}"
                      Padding="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                      MinWidth="{TemplateBinding ActualWidth}"
                      MaxHeight="{StaticResource DropDownMaxHeight}"
                      SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto">
                  <ItemsPresenter />
                </ScrollViewer>
              </Border>
            </Popup>
          </Grid>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlHover}" />
            </Trigger>
            <!-- Open is a state and has to look like one, or the only way to tell an open drop-down
                 from a closed one is the list underneath it. -->
            <Trigger Property="IsDropDownOpen" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlPressed}" />
              <Setter TargetName="Chevron" Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
              <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushDivider}" />
              <Setter TargetName="Chevron" Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
              <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
            </Trigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Choice list ============================

    The well a list of choices is read in, with a search field above it doing the finding. The catalogue
    it holds is meant to grow well past what a row of chips can carry, which is why it is a list at all.

    🔴 IT IS RECESSED: the fill goes DOWN to the base neutral, one step BELOW the card holding it. The
    first version of this part had no fill at all, on arithmetic that was half right - BrushBgSurfaceAlt
    and BrushControlRest are the SAME neutral (both Neutral03), so filling the well with the alt surface
    would have erased the selected row it exists to show. The conclusion was the wrong way out. Going
    down instead of up avoids that collision and improves every reading, measured on the render:

        selected row against the ground   1.13 -> 1.29
        hovered row against the ground    1.42 -> 1.62
        row text against the ground       7.32 -> 8.35

    Against the card it sits in this is 1.14 - the SAME step the card already takes against the window,
    which the card comment defends as what every dark interface uses for one level. With no fill the well
    was exactly 1.00 against its card, so a container and its contents were one surface and the screen
    lost a level it needed. The edge still says "you can act on this", as the card comment promises.

    🔴 THE THREE VIRTUALISATION SETTERS ARE LOAD-BEARING, not ceremony. A hand-written template drops
    what the toolkit's own ListBox style sets, and the loss is silent: a list of forty scenarios looks
    identical to a virtualised one and builds forty containers to do it. They are written here so the
    part keeps its manners at the size this catalogue is heading for.

    No focus ring on the well. The ROW carries keyboard focus and already shows it, and a second mark for
    one fact is the thing this library is trying not to do.
  -->
  <Style TargetType="ListBox">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushBgBase}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlBorder}" />
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{StaticResource ChoiceListHeight}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource SpaceXs}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.HorizontalScrollBarVisibility" Value="Disabled" />
    <Setter Property="ScrollViewer.CanContentScroll" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="VirtualizingStackPanel.IsVirtualizing" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="VirtualizingStackPanel.VirtualizationMode" Value="Recycling" />
    <Setter Property="ItemsPanel">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ItemsPanelTemplate>
          <VirtualizingStackPanel />
        </ItemsPanelTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="ListBox">
          <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                  BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                  BorderThickness="{StaticResource StrokeThinEdge}"
                  CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                  SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
            <ScrollViewer Focusable="False" Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}">
              <ItemsPresenter SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />
            </ScrollViewer>
          </Border>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
    <Style.Triggers>
      <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource BrushDivider}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
      </Trigger>
    </Style.Triggers>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Choice row ============================

    One row of a list that is chosen from. Selection is a FILL plus an accent bar down the leading edge:
    colour alone would be the only thing saying "this one", and it is the one signal a reader may not
    have. The bar is also what survives the row being hovered while another row is selected.
  -->
  <Style TargetType="ListBoxItem">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource ChipPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Stretch" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="ListBoxItem">
          <Border x:Name="Face"
                  Background="Transparent"
                  CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                  SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
            <Grid>
              <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
              </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <Border x:Name="Marker" Grid.Column="0"
                      Width="{StaticResource SelectionBarWidth}"
                      Background="{DynamicResource BrushAccent}"
                      CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusSm}"
                      Visibility="Hidden" />
              <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="1" Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}" />
            </Grid>
          </Border>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlHover}" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlRest}" />
              <Setter TargetName="Marker" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocused" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Face" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushControlHover}" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsEnabled" Value="False">
              <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
            </Trigger>
            <!-- 🔴 Selected AND disabled, the same lesson the check control already carries one part up:
                 a dead row still shouting in the accent colour is the loudest thing on a list nobody can
                 use. Caught on the render, where a fully disabled list kept a full-strength bar. -->
            <MultiTrigger>
              <MultiTrigger.Conditions>
                <Condition Property="IsSelected" Value="True" />
                <Condition Property="IsEnabled" Value="False" />
              </MultiTrigger.Conditions>
              <Setter TargetName="Marker" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushGlyphMuted}" />
            </MultiTrigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!--
    🔴 AND THE SAME ROW INSIDE A DROP-DOWN, which was missing and left every open list still wearing the
    toolkit's own item while the closed field beside it was ours. ComboBoxItem derives from ListBoxItem, so
    the drawing above applies to it unchanged - one line rather than a second copy of the same template,
    which is the difference between one thing to keep in step and two that drift.

    An implicit style keys on the exact type and does not reach a derived one, which is why this has to be
    written at all: without it the popup silently falls back past this dictionary to the toolkit's.
  -->
  <Style TargetType="ComboBoxItem" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type ListBoxItem}}" />

  <!--
    ============================ Form row ============================

    🔴 THE PART THAT FIXES "EVERYTHING LOOKS RANDOMLY PLACED". A form is a grid with a label column, not
    a stack of centred rows: when every row centres itself, the control beside a short label and the
    control beside a long one start at different x, and the eye looks for a vertical line that is not
    there. Measured on the shipped panel: 33 separate centrings, and three checkboxes whose boxes sit at
    three different x for no reason other than their labels being different lengths.

    HeaderedContentControl is used because a form row IS a header and a content: the toolkit already has
    the shape, so this is a drawing rather than a new control with its own lifetime.

    🔴 The label column has no chosen width. It is Auto with a SHARED size group, so every row on a
    screen computes one width together - as wide as the widest label that is actually there. A fixed
    width would be a number measured off today's text, and it would be wrong at the first translation.
    The screen holding the rows has to set Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True", which is the one thing a
    caller must remember; without it the rows still work and simply stop agreeing with each other.

    The message line under the control keeps its height whether it is occupied or not, so a message
    appearing does not shove the form - but only on rows that were GIVEN one. See the trigger below.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartFormRow" TargetType="HeaderedContentControl">
    <Setter Property="HorizontalContentAlignment" Value="Left" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="HeaderedContentControl">
          <Grid Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="FormLabel" />
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
              <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
              <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <ContentPresenter Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0"
                              ContentSource="Header"
                              Margin="{StaticResource FormLabelGap}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Left" />
            <ContentPresenter Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1"
                              HorizontalAlignment="{TemplateBinding HorizontalContentAlignment}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <ContentPresenter x:Name="MessageSlot" Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1"
                              Content="{Binding Tag, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}"
                              MinHeight="{StaticResource FieldMessageHeight}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <!-- 🔴 THE FLOOR IS FOR ROWS THAT CAN SPEAK, not for every row. It exists so a field whose
                 message comes and goes does not change height underneath the reader - but a row given no
                 Tag at all has no message to wait for, and reserving a line for it spent 18 px per row on
                 nothing. Measured on the setup phase: the time-zone row, the mode row and both launch
                 rows, 72 px of reserved blank for messages that cannot exist. The predicate is exactly
                 "was this row given a message slot", which is Tag being there. -->
            <Trigger Property="Tag" Value="{x:Null}">
              <Setter TargetName="MessageSlot" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </Trigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!-- The message under a field. Quiet by default, the error colour when the field is wrong - and it
       always says what to do about it, never just that something is wrong. -->
  <Style x:Key="PartFieldMessage" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource PartCaption}">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushError}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- 🔴 A FAILURE EXPLAINED IN A SENTENCE is prose, and a field message is not. PartFieldMessage is a
       caption in the error colour, which is right for a short line under the field it belongs to. The
       footer of a running session explains a command that failed mid-flight in a whole sentence, and at
       caption size that sentence took the two reductions PartNote exists to undo. The colour was never
       the problem: the error ink reads 6.02 to 1 on the footer. -->
  <Style x:Key="PartErrorNote" TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource PartNote}">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushError}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Collapsible section ============================

    A card that can be folded away, carrying a SUMMARY of what is inside it in its own header.

    🔴 The summary is the whole point, and it is what separates this from an ordinary expander. A folded
    section that says only "Speed" hides its setting and forces a click to find out; one that says
    "Speed - x60, duration scaled" answers the question without opening. That is what makes folding
    honest: nothing is hidden, only moved out of the way.

    It also answers the first-contact fault on the substitution panel, where twelve blocks all looked
    equally required and exactly one of them was.

    The header is a toggle across the full width, because a target the size of a word is a target people
    miss. The chevron turns rather than swapping glyph, so there is one drawing instead of two.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartSection" TargetType="Expander">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="Expander">
          <!-- 🔴 The card's own padding, NOT a tighter one of its own. A section and a card are peers on
               the screen, and a peer with 12 px of padding beside one with 16 starts its content at a
               different x - so the left edges of the groups did not line up, and they read as unrelated
               blocks instead of one column. One padding, one left edge. -->
          <Border Style="{StaticResource PartCard}">
            <Grid>
              <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
              </Grid.RowDefinitions>

              <Grid Grid.Row="0">
                <Border x:Name="Ring" Style="{StaticResource PartFocusRing}" Visibility="Collapsed" />
                <ToggleButton x:Name="HeaderToggle"
                              Style="{StaticResource PartDropDownToggle}"
                              IsChecked="{Binding IsExpanded, Mode=TwoWay, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}" />
                <Grid IsHitTestVisible="False">
                  <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                  </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                  <Path x:Name="Chevron" Grid.Column="0"
                        Width="{StaticResource ChevronSize}" Height="{StaticResource ChevronSize}"
                        Margin="{StaticResource SectionChevronGap}"
                        Stretch="Uniform"
                        Data="M 1,1 L 6,6 L 11,1"
                        Stroke="{DynamicResource BrushGlyphMuted}"
                        StrokeThickness="{StaticResource CheckTickStroke}"
                        StrokeStartLineCap="Round" StrokeEndLineCap="Round" StrokeLineJoin="Round"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        RenderTransformOrigin="0.5,0.5" />

                  <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="1" ContentSource="Header" VerticalAlignment="Center" />

                  <!-- The summary of what is folded away. Quiet, and on the far side of the header, so
                       the eye reads the section name first and the setting second.

                       🔴 IT SHOWS ONLY WHILE THE SECTION IS FOLDED, which is the whole of what it is for:
                       an open section already says all of this in its body, and printing it twice put the
                       same row on one screen twice over. Measured on the first render of the setup phase,
                       where the moment appeared in this header and again in the sentence above Start. -->
                  <ContentPresenter x:Name="Summary" Grid.Column="2"
                                    Content="{Binding Tag, RelativeSource={RelativeSource TemplatedParent}}"
                                    HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                </Grid>
              </Grid>

              <ContentPresenter x:Name="Body" Grid.Row="1" Margin="{StaticResource SectionBodyGap}" />
            </Grid>
          </Border>
          <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
            <Trigger Property="IsExpanded" Value="False">
              <Setter TargetName="Body" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </Trigger>
            <!-- One drawing, turned, rather than two glyphs swapped: a second glyph is a second thing
                 to keep in step, and they drift the first time one of them is resized. -->
            <Trigger Property="IsExpanded" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Chevron" Property="RenderTransform">
                <Setter.Value>
                  <RotateTransform Angle="180" />
                </Setter.Value>
              </Setter>
              <Setter TargetName="Summary" Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger SourceName="HeaderToggle" Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Chevron" Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
            </Trigger>
            <Trigger Property="IsKeyboardFocusWithin" Value="True">
              <Setter TargetName="Ring" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
            </Trigger>
          </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Verdict badge ============================

    The one place the meaning palette is allowed on something other than plain text. Colour never
    carries the meaning alone: the badge always holds a word, and the caller pairs it with a glyph.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartBadge" TargetType="Border">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource BrushBgSurfaceAlt}" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusPill}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{StaticResource ChipPadding}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Left" />
    <Setter Property="SnapsToDevicePixels" Value="True" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Side by side ============================

    Two peers in one row: equal width, apart by the channel between groups, and flush with whatever the
    row stands in. Header is the left one and Content the right one - the control's two slots, used as
    two slots.

    🔴 THE CHANNEL IS A COLUMN, NOT A MARGIN ON EACH SIDE. The first clock pair gave both clocks a 4 px
    margin all round, which was two faults at once: the gap between them came to 8 px against the 16 px
    of padding inside each card, and both outer edges stood 4 px inside the card underneath, so the page
    had two left edges. A column gives the gap once and leaves the outer edges where the container puts
    them.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartSideBySide" TargetType="HeaderedContentControl">
    <!-- A layout, so never a stop on the keyboard path. -->
    <Setter Property="Focusable" Value="False" />
    <Setter Property="IsTabStop" Value="False" />
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="HeaderedContentControl">
          <Grid>
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
              <ColumnDefinition Width="{StaticResource GroupChannelWidth}" />
              <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="0" ContentSource="Header" />
            <ContentPresenter Grid.Column="2" />
          </Grid>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Clock ============================

    One clock, drawn once and used for both of them. The fake clock and the real one are the same drawing
    with different data, so they cannot be allowed to drift apart - and the session phase shows them side
    by side, where a difference of one pixel between them reads as a difference in meaning.

    Its DataContext is a ClockView, so the template names that shape's four members and nothing else.

    🔴 THE TIME IS MONOSPACED and the shipped tile's is not. A clock at this size in a proportional face
    re-lays itself out on every tick, because a 1 is narrower than a 0 - so the digits creep sideways
    while somebody is watching them, and two clocks side by side stop lining up with each other. The date
    stays proportional: it is read once, not watched.

    🔴 Meaning comes from the role LABEL and the explicit zone, never from colour (zasady/13 section 9),
    and the zone is spelled out on every clock because untouchable rule 2 says a bare time is exactly the
    ambiguity we exist to remove.
  -->
  <ControlTemplate x:Key="PartClock">
    <Border Style="{StaticResource PartCard}">
      <StackPanel>
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}"
                   Text="{Binding RoleKey, Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}" />
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartHeading}" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   Text="{Binding Date}" />
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}"
                   FontFamily="{StaticResource FontFamilyMono}"
                   FontSize="{StaticResource FontSizeClock}"
                   FontWeight="{StaticResource FontWeightHeading}"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                   Text="{Binding Time}" />
        <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   Text="{Binding Zone}" />
        <!-- 🔴 HOW MUCH TIME PASSED ON THIS CLOCK, which is the multiplier stated as a fact. Two clocks
             showing different dates prove a substitution happened; two durations beside them - two
             minutes here, two days there - are what make the rate something a reader can see rather than
             a number they have to trust. It disappears until the first heartbeat rather than reading
             zero, because a zero beside a clock looks like a measurement. -->
        <WrapPanel Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                   Visibility="{Binding HasElapsed, Converter={StaticResource BoolToVis}}">
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                     VerticalAlignment="Center"
                     Text="{DynamicResource clock.elapsed}" />
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                     Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}"
                     FontFamily="{StaticResource FontFamilyMono}"
                     Text="{Binding Elapsed}" />
        </WrapPanel>
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </ControlTemplate>

  <!--
    ============================ Fact list ============================

    A named list of facts the session measured - covered channels and their call counts, the ones left
    real on purpose, the ones nothing was hooked in, the warnings. Read-only: there is nothing to choose
    here, which is what separates it from the choice well above. No border and no fill for the same
    reason - an edge means "you can act on this", and acting on this is not a thing.

    🔴 THE COUNT IS PART OF THE NAME, and the template composes it rather than the caller. Five of these
    appear on one screen, and a caller composing "name (n)" five times over is five chances for one of
    them to disagree with its own list. It reads the count off Items, so the number and the rows it
    describes cannot come apart.

    🔴 THE ROWS ARE MONOSPACED because they are data, not prose: an API name and a call count line up
    into columns that can be scanned, and "GetTickCount64  x44910" in a proportional face is a ragged
    edge down the middle of the list. Capped in height and scrolling, because a family session can
    produce forty channels and this is a detail inside a folded section, not the subject of the screen.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartFactList" TargetType="HeaderedItemsControl">
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="{StaticResource StackGapLg}" />
    <Setter Property="ItemTemplate">
      <Setter.Value>
        <DataTemplate>
          <!-- 🔴 THE ROWS GO THROUGH THE TRANSLATOR, and the first build of this part did not do it. The
               warning lists hold translation KEYS, so the audit rendered "wait.timeout_collapsed" on
               screen where a sentence belongs - caught on the render, after I had read past it once.

               One template for both kinds of list, because the resolver is defined to hand back a string
               it does not know (an honest "translation missing", never a blank). A covered-channel row is
               already rendered data - "GetTickCount64  ×44910" - and passes through untouched. Keys all
               carry dots and API names do not, so the two cannot be mistaken for each other. -->
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartCaption}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                     TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                     FontFamily="{StaticResource FontFamilyMono}"
                     Text="{Binding Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}" />
        </DataTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
    <Setter Property="Template">
      <Setter.Value>
        <ControlTemplate TargetType="HeaderedItemsControl">
          <StackPanel>
            <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartLabel}" TextWrapping="NoWrap"
                       TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis">
              <TextBlock.Text>
                <MultiBinding StringFormat="{}{0} ({1})">
                  <Binding Path="Header" RelativeSource="{RelativeSource TemplatedParent}" />
                  <Binding Path="Items.Count" RelativeSource="{RelativeSource TemplatedParent}" />
                </MultiBinding>
              </TextBlock.Text>
            </TextBlock>
            <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                          HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                          MaxHeight="{StaticResource CoverageMaxHeight}"
                          Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                          Focusable="False">
              <ItemsPresenter />
            </ScrollViewer>
          </StackPanel>
        </ControlTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

  <!--
    ============================ Note list ============================

    The same named list, for rows that are PROSE rather than data - the warnings a session raises.

    🔴 ONE OVERRIDDEN TEMPLATE, NOT A SECOND LIST. Everything about it is the fact list: the header, the
    composed count, the cap, the scrolling. What changes is the row, and it changes because the content is
    a different kind. A channel row is "GetTickCount64  ×44910" - data in two columns, which monospace
    lines up and a clipped tail costs nothing. A warning is a sentence, and a sentence clipped to
    "...ran at the shortest step this tool uses (1 ms) inste…" is one the reader cannot act on, which is
    the whole point of having raised it.

    Measured on the session-audit render: both warnings ran past the card and lost their ends.
  -->
  <Style x:Key="PartNoteList" TargetType="HeaderedItemsControl"
         BasedOn="{StaticResource PartFactList}">
    <Setter Property="ItemTemplate">
      <Setter.Value>
        <DataTemplate>
          <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource PartNote}"
                     Margin="{StaticResource StackGapSm}"
                     Text="{Binding Converter={StaticResource KeyToText}}" />
        </DataTemplate>
      </Setter.Value>
    </Setter>
  </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
